```kotlin
// Declaration-site: the class itself declares its variance, once
class Box<out T>(val value: T)   // 'out' = covariant: Box<Dog> is-a Box<Animal>
fun printAnimal(box: Box<Animal>) { println(box.value) }
printAnimal(Box<Dog>(myDog))     // legal -- Box<T> is declared covariant
```

```java
// Use-site: each usage site declares the variance it needs
void printAnimal(Box<? extends Animal> box) { System.out.println(box.get()); }
// '? extends Animal' means: some specific but unknown subtype of Animal
printAnimal(new Box<Dog>(myDog));  // legal -- the wildcard grants covariance here
```

Kotlin's `out T` on the class declaration commits `Box` to being covariant *everywhere it's ever used* — one decision, made once, by whoever writes the class. Java's `? extends Animal` instead grants covariance *only at this specific use site* — the class `Box<T>` itself stays plain and invariant, and any caller who wants covariant behavior asks for it explicitly with a wildcard, one call site at a time. Neither is more "correct" — declaration-site variance is simpler to reason about once (read the class, know its variance everywhere); use-site variance is more flexible per call but requires re-deciding at every site that needs it.
